  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 233 PM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\n**** A Few Showers Tonight into Saturday Morning ****\n\n18z/1pm surface analysis shows a nearly stationary frontal\nboundary extending from near Kansas City into the Tennessee \nRiver Valley. A short-wave trough dropping southeastward out of\nthe Upper Midwest is currently interacting with the boundary to\nproduce clouds and a few radar echoes across central Illinois.\nDespite the radar presentation, only a few light sprinkles have\nreached the ground in spots...as the 12z KILX upper air sounding\nindicated ample dry air below 600mb. Forecast soundings show the\natmosphere slowly moistening from the top-down, which will\neventually allow showers to become more numerous late tonight\ninto Saturday morning before the short-wave sinks into the Ohio\nRiver Valley. Most CAMs suggest scattered sprinkles this\nafternoon, followed by a lull in precip until late tonight when\nthe 850mb jet streak modestly increases to 20-30kt. Scattered\nshowers will develop...mainly along/south of a Rushville to\nLawrenceville line. May even see a rumble of thunder or two as \nMUCAPEs reach 1000-1500J/kg. Once the wave passes, short-wave\nridging in its wake will lead to an end to the light showers by\nmidday Saturday, followed by a partly to mostly cloudy and dry\nafternoon.\n\n\n**** Typical Summertime Heat Returns ****\n\nUpper ridging currently centered over the Desert Southwest will\nbuild northeastward early next week: however, its progress will\nbe curtailed by a deep low over Hudson Bay. Pieces of energy\npivoting around the low will drop into the eastern Great Lakes\nand New England...maintaining the northwesterly flow pattern\nacross the Midwest. As upper heights rise, temperatures will\nclimb into the upper 80s and lower 90s on Sunday, then into the\nlower to middle 90s on Monday. With dewpoints rising back into\nthe 70s, heat index readings will reach 100 degrees or higher\nacross the entire CWA by Sunday afternoon. Things get a little\nmore interesting on Monday as a convective complex impacts parts\nof the region. Confidence on the exact track/timing of the\ncomplex remains low, but the synoptic pattern and early signs\nfrom some of the longer-range CAMs such as the RRFS suggest the\nstorms may only graze central Illinois to the northeast. This\ncould potentially send an outflow boundary W/SW and impede\nheating across the E/NE KILX CWA on Monday. The 00z Jul 24 LREF\nbacks up this theory as it shows only a 20% chance or less of\nheat index values exceeding 105 degrees east of the I-57\ncorridor Monday afternoon. As such, decided to issue a Heat\nAdvisory across all locations west of I-57 for Sunday afternoon\nthrough Monday evening. Future shifts may opt to expand the\nadvisory further east across part or all of east-central\nIllinois depending on convective trends.\n\n\n**** Severe Weather Potential Sunday Night into Monday ****\n\nThe latest convective outlooks from SPC have pulled the severe\nweather risk area a bit further northeast than previously\nthought. Despite rising upper heights, the flow across the\nMidwest/Great Lakes will remain northwesterly. As such, will\nneed to keep a close eye on the track/timing of short-wave\ntroughs cresting the ridge, then digging southeastward through\nthe Great Lakes. Its still too early to pinpoint this process:\nhowever, the 06z/12z RRFS offers an early glance at a potential\nscenario featuring a wave triggering a storm cluster across \ncentral Wisconsin late Sunday night dropping southeastward \nacross Lake Michigan into northern/central Indiana Monday \nmorning. Previous runs of the RRFS showed this convection \ndirectly impacting the NE KILX CWA around Champaign/Danville:\nhowever, the 12z run is further east and much less impressive. \nEven if the initial storm cluster completely misses central \nIllinois to the northeast, the residual outflow boundary could \nserve as the focus for convective re-development across the E/NE\nCWA Monday afternoon/evening. With SBCAPEs in excess of \n3000J/kg and 0-6km bulk shear of 30-40kt, any cells that form in\nthis environment will be capable of damaging wind gusts and \nlarge hail and perhaps a couple tornadoes. Stay tuned to later \nforecasts for additional details.",
